# Marin Coding V0 (`marin code`), 2026-10-19

A read-only coding assistant that is a client of `marin serve`. Machine and model: Ryzen 7 260, RTX 5050
Laptop (40 W), Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA, server `--ctx 8192 --temp 0`, one client at a time. Tasks run on Marin's own
repository (the working tree at this commit plus the new `code` module, which the model could read too).

## What was built

- `marin code [PATH] [--port 8000] [--log FILE]`: terminal conversation. Interactive on a TTY (line editor, history,
  markdown answers, quiet `›` tool lines, a footer with tools / tokens / context / seconds); one question per line
  when stdin is piped, with a non-zero exit if a question failed.
- Client in std only: HTTP/1.1 with a reader thread for the chunked server-sent events, so Ctrl-C hangs up within a
  tick; connect timeout 3 s, silence timeout 240 s; the server's `marin_session_id` is reused so each request only
  reads the new part of the conversation.
- Server changes (the only runtime change, needed by the client): `/v1/models` reports `context_window`, and the final
  streamed chunk carries `usage` (prompt and completion tokens). The client needs both to manage the window.
- Tools (`list_files(path, depth<=3)`, `read_file(path, start, end)`, `search(pattern, path)`), confined to the project:
  every path is resolved with symlinks followed and must stay inside the root; search never follows links, skips
  `.git`, `target`, `node_modules`, binary, non-UTF-8 and >1 MiB files; one result is capped at context/2 characters
  (1500-12000), a listing at 100 entries, a read at 250 lines (with the line to continue from), a search at 40
  matches within a 4 s budget; `<tool_` markup in file text is broken up so it cannot pass for protocol.
- Tool loop: the model writes Qwen's native `<tool_call><function=..><parameter=..>` form; the client parses it,
  runs it, and sends the result back as `<tool_response>` in a user message. At most 8 rounds per question, 4 calls
  per reply; then the model is told no more tools will run and must answer. Results always come from the project.
- Context: token cost per character is calibrated from the server's counts; before each request old tool output is
  replaced by a one-line stub, then the oldest whole questions are dropped (with a visible note); a question that
  cannot fit is refused. A failed or cancelled question is removed from the conversation.

## Harness history (evidence for every change; all changes are generic, none mention a task)

| Run | What happened on the five tasks | Change made |
|---|---|---|
| r1 first harness | t1 ok; t2 `list .` eight times in a row, then "no access to the code"; t3 ok; t4 wrong guess `src`, then a runaway `</path>` x1000 until the token limit, gave up; t5 ok | stop streaming a call that runs past 1000 characters; refuse an exact repeat with an error; `list_files` depth; top-level listing in the system prompt; `a\|b` in search; end tool use with a final "answer now" request instead of failing the question |
| r2-r4 | model wrote `<path>x</parameter>` and `<parameter=start>1` without closing tags | specific error text ("it had only: start"); a dropped `parameter=` prefix is read and counted as a repair; a call that rambles on after its parameters is read and kept in proper form |
| x1 (12 more tasks) 8 of 17 | model kept writing `foo.*bar`; literal search returned "no matches"; the model concluded the code was missing (a2, a7, a8, a12) | `.*` as an in-order wildcard, documented; a no-match on regex syntax says so |
| y1, y1b, h1 | final harness; h1 written and rubric-fixed before it ran, no changes afterwards | none |

